# Single-page app fallback.
#
# ⚠ INERT ON THE CURRENT HOST. descry.in is a Render static site configured
# through the dashboard, and Render reads neither this file nor render.yaml for
# a dashboard-managed service — the authoritative rules live in
# Settings → Redirects/Rewrites, and this file is served as a plain asset.
# It is kept because it is the same ruleset in portable form: if the site ever
# moves to Netlify or Cloudflare Pages it starts working with no changes, and
# it documents what the dashboard is expected to contain. Keep the two in sync.
#
# The portal addresses itself with REAL paths via history.pushState (/brief,
# /story/<id>, /trend/<id>, /signal/<id>, /trends, /saved, /read, /profile) —
# deliberately, so search engines and shared links see one canonical URL per
# story instead of everything collapsing to "#".
#
# The cost is that the host must hand back index.html for any path it has no
# file for. Client-side routing only starts AFTER index.html loads, so on a
# refresh or a pasted link the browser asks the host for /brief directly; with
# no rule here that is a 404 from the host ("Cannot GET /brief") and the app
# never boots. It bites logged-out visitors hardest because they are the ones
# arriving on shared links rather than already having a tab open.
#
# Status 200 (not 301/302): rewrite, don't redirect, so the address bar keeps
# the real URL for the router to read. Real files — index.html, icon.svg,
# og.png, robots.txt — are served normally and still take precedence.
#
# Read by Netlify, Cloudflare Pages and Render static sites. Other hosts need
# the same rule expressed their own way:
#   Vercel   vercel.json -> {"rewrites":[{"source":"/(.*)","destination":"/index.html"}]}
#   Express  app.get('*', (req,res) => res.sendFile(__dirname + '/index.html'))
#   nginx    location / { try_files $uri $uri/ /index.html; }
#   serve    serve -s web        (the -s flag IS this rule)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- share cards
# Shared links live on THIS domain (descry.in/s/<id>) so what people paste into
# WhatsApp or Slack carries the product's name, not the API's hostname. The
# server-rendered Open Graph card for a story — its real headline, description
# and photograph — only exists on the API, so these three paths proxy through
# to it. Status 200 = proxy, not redirect: the crawler must receive that HTML
# under this domain, because a 301 to another host is where several unfurlers
# stop following.
#
# MUST come before the /* rule below — first match wins, and the catch-all
# would otherwise swallow these and hand back index.html (generic site card).
#
# If a host cannot proxy to an external origin (Cloudflare Pages cannot; Netlify
# and Render can), delete these three lines: links keep working — index.html is
# served and the SPA maps /s/<id> to /story/<id> — but previews fall back to the
# site card. The alternative that needs no proxy is pointing a subdomain such as
# link.descry.in at the API and setting SHARE_ORIGIN to it.
/s/*  https://newslens-rmv6.onrender.com/s/:splat  200
/t/*  https://newslens-rmv6.onrender.com/t/:splat  200
/g/*  https://newslens-rmv6.onrender.com/g/:splat  200

# ------------------------------------------------------ server-rendered pages
# The indexable pages. Without these, every /story/<id> is the same empty
# index.html until JavaScript runs, and a crawler that does not wait for it sees
# one generic page at every URL. The API returns the SAME app with this page's
# title, description, canonical, structured data and article text already in it
# (backend/app/pages.py), and a real 404 for anything that no longer exists.
# Also first-match-wins, so these must stay above the catch-all.
/story/*   https://newslens-rmv6.onrender.com/page/story/:splat   200
/trend/*   https://newslens-rmv6.onrender.com/page/trend/:splat   200
/signal/*  https://newslens-rmv6.onrender.com/page/signal/:splat  200

# The sitemaps, on the site's own domain. robots.txt names the API-host copies,
# which work without these rules; these make the conventional URLs work too.
/sitemap.xml       https://newslens-rmv6.onrender.com/sitemap.xml       200
/news-sitemap.xml  https://newslens-rmv6.onrender.com/news-sitemap.xml  200

/*    /index.html   200
